Cleft lip and palate
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Visible upper lip fissure, Palatal defect, Nasal regurgitation during feeding, Hypernasal speech

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-012
Reason for Visit: Acute feeding intolerance, suspected dehydration, and high fever.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Infant and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 4-month-old infant with a known un-repaired cleft palate presenting with 24 hours of total refusal to feed, frequent nasal regurgitation of fluids, high fever, and decreased urine output. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the high risk of rapid dehydration and aspiration pneumonia in a vulnerable infant with compromised upper airway anatomy.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Milk keeps pouring out of his nose when he tries to drink, and his diapers have been completely dry since yesterday afternoon.
History of Present Illness: The parents report a 24-hour history of severe feeding difficulties with the infant violently crying and refusing the specialized cleft bottle. When feeds are forced, there is immediate nasal regurgitation during feeding. A high fever developed 6 hours ago. The infant has an unrepaired palatal defect and visible upper lip fissure. The parents note poor weight gain historically, but today the infant is lethargic with no wet nappies for 14 hours. The patient has a history of hypernasal speech precursors (vocalizations) but is currently exhibiting weak, hoarse crying.
Functional Status: Fully dependent infant; currently unable to maintain oral hydration.
Social/Safety Concerns: High parental anxiety and exhaustion; immediate risk of severe infant dehydration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/50 mmHg | HR: 165 bpm | Temp: 39.1 C | RR: 45 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 5.2 Kg
General: Infant is lethargic, mottled, and hot to touch. Cry is weak and hoarse.
Physical Exam: Head and neck exam reveals a visible unilateral left-sided upper lip fissure extending to the nostril and a large palatal defect communicating directly with the nasal cavity. Anterior fontanelle is sunken. Mucous membranes are tacky with no tears on crying. Capillary refill is 3-4 seconds. Tympanic membranes are bulging and erythematous bilaterally, with purulent effusion visible on the right. Lung fields are clear with no focal crackles or wheeze.
Environmental Observations: Multiple types of specialized teats and bottles scattered, indicating prolonged, failed feeding attempts.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications; parents administered infant paracetamol 4 hours ago with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cleft lip and palate (complicated by acute bilateral otitis media and moderate dehydration) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Aspiration Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of focal lung crackles, normal oxygen saturation on room air, and no sustained work of breathing between crying episodes.

Viral Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the complete absence of vomiting or diarrhea; the fluid loss is purely due to failure of intake and fever.

Urinary Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause of fever, given the obvious source of infection visualized in the middle ear, a common complication of Eustachian tube dysfunction in cleft palate.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Risk of rapid physiological decompensation)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of high fever, lethargy, and anuria in the context of a known cleft lip and palate represents a pediatric emergency. The objective findings of tachycardia, sunken fontanelle, and prolonged capillary refill confirmed moderate clinical dehydration, secondary to severe acute otitis media causing severe feeding pain and refusal. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because infants with palatal defects cannot compensate for fluid deficits, and immediate initiation of oral rehydration therapy and antibiotic treatment was critical to prevent progression to severe hypovolemic shock requiring hospital admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered weight-based dose of oral Ibuprofen to reduce fever and analgesize the middle ear. Guided parents in administering oral rehydration solution (ORS) 5ml every 5 minutes via oral syringe directed to the side of the cheek to bypass the palatal defect. Prescribed Amoxicillin oral suspension for otitis media.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you administer fluids in 5ml micro-boluses with the syringe, the infant can swallow without it entering the nasal cavity; therefore, strictly adhering to this slow syringe method ensures successful hydration. When you track every wet nappy on a chart, you visually confirm his recovery; maintaining this chart rewards your efforts and provides critical data for the GP.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a lactation consultant specialized in cleft palate feeding.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided a calibrated oral syringe and pediatric ORS sachets.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 12 hours to verify adequate urine output and hydration status.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Take the infant directly to the nearest Paediatric Emergency Department or call 000 if he becomes completely floppy and unresponsive, if he vomits the fluid continuously, or if there is no wet nappy in the next 6 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Resolution of the infection will take 48-72 hours; syringe feeding must be maintained until the infant accepts the bottle again.
Clinical Course: The fever and pain from the ear infection will begin to subside after 24 hours of antibiotics and regular ibuprofen, which will gradually improve feeding tolerance.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek urgent medical review if the infant develops a rash that does not fade under pressure, or if his breathing becomes very rapid and shallow while resting.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The ear infection is causing intense pain when he tries to suck, leading to the feeding refusal and dehydration. The ibuprofen stops the pain, and the tiny syringe drops allow him to absorb fluid without using the painful sucking motion.
Actionable Steps: Give 5ml of ORS every 5 minutes for the next 2 hours. Give Ibuprofen every 6 hours. Chart all wet nappies. Start antibiotics immediately.

